WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.637
Let's extend our dynamic array with four extra operations: pop by index,

2
00:00:05.637 --> 00:00:08.455
contains, insert, and remove by value.

3
00:00:08.855 --> 00:00:13.417
The implementation builds on the dynamic array from Problem twenty-five point one.

4
00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:18.972
We already have append, get, set, size, and pop back.

5
00:00:19.497 --> 00:00:23.786
We can combine those methods to implement the new operations without accessing

6
00:00:23.786 --> 00:00:25.215
the backing array directly.

7
00:00:25.615 --> 00:00:30.064
First, remember the difference between worst-case and amortized time.

8
00:00:30.581 --> 00:00:33.505
Append and pop back usually take constant time.

9
00:00:34.041 --> 00:00:38.512
A resize can make one operation take linear time, but doubling when

10
00:00:38.512 --> 00:00:42.984
full and shrinking below one quarter full keep their amortized cost constant.

11
00:00:43.487 --> 00:00:47.560
Storage takes linear space in the number of live elements, with a

12
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.917
minimum of ten slots.

13
00:00:49.427 --> 00:00:53.253
During resizing, both arrays temporarily coexist.

14
00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:55.497
Now, pop by index.

15
00:00:55.982 --> 00:01:00.391
This operation removes an element at a specific index and returns its

16
00:01:00.391 --> 00:01:00.758
value.

17
00:01:01.295 --> 00:01:05.354
The index must be at least zero and strictly less than size.

18
00:01:05.856 --> 00:01:08.260
An empty array has no valid index.

19
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.433
Save the value at the requested index before changing anything.

20
00:01:12.921 --> 00:01:16.144
Then shift every later element one slot to the left.

21
00:01:16.664 --> 00:01:21.572
Finally, call pop back to reduce size and handle shrinking, and return

22
00:01:21.572 --> 00:01:22.799
the saved value.

23
00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:30.022
For example, start with three, one, four, one, five, nine, two.

24
00:01:30.497 --> 00:01:32.578
Popping index two removes four.

25
00:01:33.061 --> 00:01:37.623
Move one, five, nine, and two left by one position.

26
00:01:38.142 --> 00:01:43.410
The result is three, one, one, five, nine, two.

27
00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:48.155
Size drops from seven to six, and capacity stays ten.

28
00:01:48.555 --> 00:01:52.696
The shift loop moves forward, from the removed index toward the end.

29
00:01:53.197 --> 00:01:56.607
Each assignment copies the next element into the current slot.

30
00:01:57.155 --> 00:02:01.273
Because we already saved the removed value, it is safe to overwrite

31
00:02:01.273 --> 00:02:01.960
that slot.

32
00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.915
Popping the last index requires no shifts, so it behaves like pop

33
00:02:06.915 --> 00:02:07.295
back.

34
00:02:07.809 --> 00:02:12.784
Popping the first index shifts all the remaining elements, giving linear worst-case

35
00:02:12.784 --> 00:02:13.199
time.

36
00:02:13.726 --> 00:02:18.377
General pop by index also has linear amortized time: repeatedly removing from

37
00:02:18.377 --> 00:02:23.029
the front requires a total number of shifts proportional to n squared.

38
00:02:23.551 --> 00:02:27.848
If you frequently remove from both ends, consider a double-ended queue.

39
00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:29.947
Second, contains.

40
00:02:30.439 --> 00:02:34.544
This method returns true when a value appears in the array, and

41
00:02:34.544 --> 00:02:35.228
false otherwise.

42
00:02:35.715 --> 00:02:39.209
Scan the live indices from zero to size minus one.

43
00:02:39.706 --> 00:02:43.506
Compare each element with the target and return true at the first

44
00:02:43.506 --> 00:02:43.823
match.

45
00:02:44.369 --> 00:02:47.955
If the scan finishes without a match, return false.

46
00:02:48.355 --> 00:02:52.390
For an empty array, the loop does no work and returns false.

47
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.961
A match at the first index takes constant time.

48
00:02:56.482 --> 00:03:00.831
A missing value, or a match at the last index, requires scanning

49
00:03:00.831 --> 00:03:04.455
all n elements, so the worst case is linear time.

50
00:03:04.976 --> 00:03:09.474
Do not search the unused capacity: those slots are outside the logical

51
00:03:09.474 --> 00:03:09.849
array.

52
00:03:10.249 --> 00:03:11.566
Third, insert.

53
00:03:12.102 --> 00:03:15.118
This operation adds a value at a chosen index.

54
00:03:15.637 --> 00:03:21.252
Unlike get, set, and pop, insertion allows an index equal to size.

55
00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.093
That position means inserting at the end.

56
00:03:24.587 --> 00:03:29.488
Therefore, valid indices range from zero through size, inclusive.

57
00:03:29.966 --> 00:03:33.007
For an empty array, index zero is valid.

58
00:03:33.407 --> 00:03:35.819
Make room by appending a temporary zero.

59
00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:40.303
Append handles any necessary growth and increases size by one.

60
00:03:40.809 --> 00:03:45.181
Then shift the existing elements right, working backward from the new last

61
00:03:45.181 --> 00:03:47.002
index toward the insertion point.

62
00:03:47.517 --> 00:03:51.085
Finally, set the requested index to the new value.

63
00:03:51.485 --> 00:03:52.533
Why move backward?

64
00:03:53.068 --> 00:03:57.162
If we shifted right while moving forward, we would overwrite an element

65
00:03:57.162 --> 00:03:58.186
before copying it.

66
00:03:58.715 --> 00:04:02.087
Moving from right to left preserves each value until it has been

67
00:04:02.087 --> 00:04:02.368
moved.

68
00:04:02.768 --> 00:04:07.452
For example, insert eight at index one into three, one, four.

69
00:04:07.953 --> 00:04:11.200
First append zero, making four live slots.

70
00:04:11.697 --> 00:04:14.300
Move four right, then move one right.

71
00:04:14.838 --> 00:04:16.453
Write eight at index one.

72
00:04:16.957 --> 00:04:20.419
The result is three, eight, one, four.

73
00:04:20.819 --> 00:04:24.155
Insertion at the beginning shifts all n existing elements.

74
00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:27.053
A resize can also copy the array.

75
00:04:27.546 --> 00:04:29.374
The worst-case time is linear.

76
00:04:29.896 --> 00:04:34.078
Insertion at the end requires no shifts and has the same constant

77
00:04:34.078 --> 00:04:35.472
amortized cost as append.

78
00:04:35.999 --> 00:04:41.314
General insertion remains linear even with amortized analysis because repeated insertions at

79
00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:43.529
the front always require shifting.

80
00:04:43.929 --> 00:04:46.043
Fourth, remove by value.

81
00:04:46.543 --> 00:04:49.749
Pop uses an index; remove searches for a value.

82
00:04:50.293 --> 00:04:54.018
Remove only the first occurrence and return its original index.

83
00:04:54.553 --> 00:04:57.702
If the value is absent, return minus one.

84
00:04:58.102 --> 00:04:59.585
Scan from left to right.

85
00:05:00.101 --> 00:05:04.283
When a matching value is found, call pop at that index and

86
00:05:04.283 --> 00:05:05.677
return the index immediately.

87
00:05:06.194 --> 00:05:12.199
For example, removing one from three, one, four, one returns index one

88
00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:14.702
and leaves three, four, one.

89
00:05:15.205 --> 00:05:17.198
The later occurrence stays in the array.

90
00:05:17.599 --> 00:05:21.849
Searching takes up to linear time, and shifting also takes up to

91
00:05:21.849 --> 00:05:22.558
linear time.

92
00:05:23.051 --> 00:05:27.411
Adding those costs still gives linear time, not quadratic time.

93
00:05:27.916 --> 00:05:31.904
Contains alone is not sufficient for this implementation because its boolean result

94
00:05:31.904 --> 00:05:34.562
does not tell us where the match occurred.

95
00:05:34.962 --> 00:05:39.519
All four extra methods use constant auxiliary space apart from any resizing

96
00:05:39.519 --> 00:05:41.798
performed by append or pop back.

97
00:05:42.333 --> 00:05:46.051
A resize can temporarily require linear extra space.

98
00:05:46.451 --> 00:05:48.024
Here are the key takeaways.

99
00:05:48.526 --> 00:05:52.205
Get, set, and size take constant time.

100
00:05:52.726 --> 00:05:55.978
Append and pop back take constant amortized time.

101
00:05:56.473 --> 00:06:01.336
Pop by index, contains, insert, and remove by value take linear time

102
00:06:01.336 --> 00:06:02.957
in the worst case.

103
00:06:03.465 --> 00:06:08.505
Dynamic resizing manages capacity, but it does not eliminate the cost of

104
00:06:08.505 --> 00:06:10.184
searching or shifting elements.

105
00:06:10.584 --> 00:06:16.072
These techniques are building blocks for stacks, heaps, hash sets, and maps.

106
00:06:16.566 --> 00:06:21.610
The chapter's online resources include practice with an AI interviewer and full

107
00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:23.712
code solutions in multiple languages.
